From 16b4201f9a73a2e63702eac2303b6d8b8615c04d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: woahwhattheheck <293286387+woahwhattheheck@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 04:33:18 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] pulse-server: apply filters requested with
 filter.want/filter.apply

Do what the PulseAudio module-filter-heuristics and module-filter-apply
modules do: when a stream asks for a filter with the filter.want or
filter.apply property, load module-<filter> on top of the device the
stream uses, move the stream to the filter and unload the filter again
10 seconds after it is no longer used. filter.suppress disables the
filter for a stream and filter.apply.<filter>.parameters adds module
arguments.

Echo cancellation needs a playback and a record stream. Like
PulseAudio, it is applied when a playback and a record stream of the
same group (media.role, application.id, application.name or media.name)
both want it. Both streams are then routed through one
module-echo-cancel that uses their devices as sink_master and
source_master. filter.want is ignored on devices meant for calls
(device.intended-roles with phone or Communication), filter.apply is
always honoured.

Streams are moved with the target.object metadata, like a move with
pactl. The streams get state.restore-target = false while they are
moved to a filter so that the session manager does not remember the
filter as their target. When the session manager did not link a moved
stream to the filter after 2 seconds, the move is requested again.

When someone else moves a filtered stream to another device, the filter
is applied on the new device. When the device of a filter goes away,
the filter is unloaded and the streams are filtered again where they
end up. When the filter module is unloaded by hand, the streams go back
to their devices and the filter is not loaded again for them.

This gives communication applications that use the PulseAudio API,
such as Jami, echo cancellation without any configuration.

Automatic filters can be disabled with pulse.filter-apply = false.

Fixes #4170
